WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
In the pulmonary system, an open alveolus provides a

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.122
surface for gas exchange.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.560
Type one alveolar cells provide structure.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.392
Type two cells secrete surfactant.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.200
Notice the inner air surface during expiration.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.980
Surfactant lowers surface tension
and helps prevent collapse.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.980
Without enough surfactant, alveoli
can collapse and ventilation falls.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.780
This is especially important
in premature infants whose lungs

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.498
have not developed adequate production and secretion.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.616
Now follow oxygen across
the alveolar and capillary walls.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.180
Diffusion depends on a thin membrane, a large surface

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.383
area, and a partial pressure gradient.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.274
Blood flow then carries oxygen toward the tissues.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.520
Ventilation, diffusion, and perfusion
are related, but they are

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.220
different steps.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.350
A change in one step can disrupt exchange even

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.773
when the others remain intact.
